       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter amlodipine 30 mg daily. It's three times the maximum labeled dose, and your confirmation doesn't change that. You haven't given a clinical reason for it, and you haven't said an attending or resident has approved it. Nothing in the patient details you gave (70 kg, normal kidney and liver function, no allergies) would justify it either. I can't see a way for this dose to help the patient, and it could cause serious harm.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** I can enter 5 mg or 10 mg by mouth once daily. Tell me which one.\n- **Escalate the 30 mg question.** If your attending or resident really wants 30 mg, they should enter and sign that order themselves. The pharmacist will also review it and may call to clarify.\n\nIf the goal is more blood pressure control than 10 mg gives, the usual step is to add a second agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, an ARB, or a thiazide, instead of going above 10 mg of amlodipine. That's worth raising on rounds.\n\nWhich standard dose should I enter?"
      }
     ],
